WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.105 --> 00:00:01.270
(upbeat music)

00:00:01.270 --> 00:00:03.140
- [Narrator] The first
thing about Mexico City

00:00:03.140 --> 00:00:05.773
that will grab you is the
city's immense sprawl.

00:00:07.190 --> 00:00:09.121
The second thing is the food.

00:00:09.121 --> 00:00:12.350
(upbeat music)

00:00:12.350 --> 00:00:15.160
The street food of Mexico
City reflects the ingredients

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:17.550
of the entire country and history

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:19.350
that spans hundreds of years.

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:21.140
(upbeat music)

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:22.560
In the neighborhood of Juárez,

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:24.940
you can find an ingredient
that at first glance,

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:27.970
may seem off-putting; but,
it turns out, corn fungus

00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:30.331
is a Mexican flavor to be reckoned with.

00:00:30.331 --> 00:00:32.914
(upbeat music)

00:00:35.223 --> 00:00:38.306
(majestic music)

00:00:38.306 --> 00:00:40.466
(calm, soothing music)

00:00:40.466 --> 00:00:44.966
(Nicolasa speaks in foreign language)

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:37.140
Head toward the Anthropology Museum

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:39.600
and down a pedestrian
walk, and you'll find Ana,

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:41.500
selling Dorilocos, a dish

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:43.599
that certainly lives up to its name.

00:01:43.599 --> 00:01:46.099
(chill music)

00:01:47.503 --> 00:01:51.586
(Ana speaks in foreign language)

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:34.510
(chill music)

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:38.610
A 10 minute cab ride
later can find you eating

00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:41.530
some of the best octopus you've ever had.

00:02:41.530 --> 00:02:42.810
You can't miss this vendor

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:45.314
because his cart takes
up half the sidewalk.

00:02:45.314 --> 00:02:47.897
(upbeat music)

00:02:49.995 --> 00:02:54.245
(Diego speaks in foreign language)

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:40.770
In the neighborhood of Hipódromo,

00:03:40.770 --> 00:03:43.620
there is one street corner you can't miss

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:45.790
because the tortas here
are said to be some

00:03:45.790 --> 00:03:49.673
of the best in the city.
(upbeat music)

00:03:50.865 --> 00:03:55.115
(Perla speaks in foreign language)

00:04:42.446 --> 00:04:45.010
(upbeat music)

00:04:45.010 --> 00:04:47.070
Down in the Historic
Center, there is a block

00:04:47.070 --> 00:04:49.550
jam-packed with vendors, but B-line it

00:04:49.550 --> 00:04:52.170
to Fany's stand for her
take on the tlacoyo.

00:04:52.170 --> 00:04:54.223
It's a traditional Mexican street snack.

00:04:54.223 --> 00:04:56.806
(upbeat music)

00:04:58.093 --> 00:05:02.426
(Fany speaks in a foreign language)

00:05:42.645 --> 00:05:45.058
(Fany laughs)

00:05:45.058 --> 00:05:46.266
(chill guitar music)

00:05:46.266 --> 00:05:48.130
And finally, back in Juárez,

00:05:48.130 --> 00:05:50.270
a man named Francisco Solis has a stand

00:05:50.270 --> 00:05:52.930
with a few stools and a
dish that you can smell

00:05:52.930 --> 00:05:56.923
from blocks away.
(chill guitar music)

00:05:58.689 --> 00:06:03.272
(Francisco speaks in foreign language)

00:06:50.889 --> 00:06:53.472
(upbeat music)

